  {
   "topic": "Two tolerance series",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "At the same nominal voltage the B types have a narrower VZ window than the C types: for 12 V, 11.8 V to 12.2 V (BZX84B12LT1G) against 11.4 V to 12.7 V (BZX84C12LT1G), both at 5 mA. For a given voltage the two series list the same impedance, leakage, temperature-coefficient and capacitance limits."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Test currents",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "C types up to 24 V are specified at three currents: 1 mA, 5 mA (the main point, IZT1) and 20 mA. From 27 V upward the three points drop to 0.1 mA (ZZT2 at 0.5 mA), 2 mA and 10 mA. The B types use one point: 5 mA up to 24 V and 2 mA for 27 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Impedance against voltage",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Dynamic impedance at 5 mA is lowest, 10 Ω max, for the 6.2 V type and rises toward both ends: 100 Ω for 2.4 V and 255 Ω for 75 V at 2 mA. At 1 mA the low-voltage types reach 600 Ω. The typical curves show the same dip near 5 V to 6 V and a second minimum near 12 V to 16 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature coefficient",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Types up to 4.3 V have a coefficient between -3.5 and 0 mV/K; 4.7 V to 5.6 V span zero; from 6.2 V up it is positive and grows with voltage, to 73.4 to 88.6 mV/K for 75 V. The typical curve crosses zero near 4.3 V and reads about 7 mV/°C at 11 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage and temperature",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Typical leakage is tens of microamps for the lowest-voltage types and falls steeply to a flat level above about 7 V: near 0.0006 µA at +25 °C, 0.0003 µA at -55 °C and about 0.065 µA at +150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Assembly and construction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The case is thermoset plastic, transfer molded with no voids, flammability class UL 94 V-0; the leads carry a finish that resists corrosion and solders readily. The case may reach 260 °C for 10 s during soldering. A band marks the cathode. Pb-free and RoHS compliant."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package dimensions",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Dimensioning and tolerancing follow ASME Y14.5M-1994; millimetres control. Body length D and width E exclude mold flash, protrusions and gate burrs. The maximum lead thickness includes the lead finish; the minimum is the base metal alone."
  }
 ],
